From 0046e0000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zhymabek Roman <robanokssamit@yandex.ru>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui, twinapi.appcore, windows.storage, windows.web,
 wintypes, coremessaging: Report E_NOINTERFACE from WinRT activation factories
 for classes a module does not implement, and implement the windowing-internal
 window/display/icon identifiers

Every WinRT module on Windows is built by the C++/WinRT projection, and the
DllGetActivationFactory() that the generated module boilerplate produces answers
a class identifier it has no runtime class for with E_NOINTERFACE (0x80004002) -
the generated get_activation_factory() template throws hresult_no_interface()
for a name that is not in its class table.  The Wine implementations of the 39
WinRT modules answered with CLASS_E_CLASSNOTAVAILABLE (0x80040111), the
DllGetClassObject() value for a CLSID, so any caller that distinguishes the two
was told "this module cannot produce that class at all" where Windows says
"this module is not the one that serves it".

The Windows App SDK depends on that difference.  Its Microsoft.UI.Windowing.Core
.dll entry point is a chain:

    Core::Logging::ReunionModuleDllGetActivationFactory( classId );
    if (!IsApiSetImplemented( "ext-ms-win-windowing-internal-l1-1-0" ))
        return CFlat::GetActivationFactory( classId );        /* the WinAppSDK class table */
    result = Windowing_DllGetActivationFactory( classId );     /* the system windowing module */
    if (result == E_NOINTERFACE)
        return CFlat::GetActivationFactory( classId );        /* fall back */
    return result;

Wine routes ext-ms-win-windowing-internal-l1-1-0 to windows.ui.dll and exports
Windowing_DllGetActivationFactory from it, so windows.ui.dll is asked about every
Microsoft.UI.Windowing class: AppWindow, AppWindowTitleBar, OverlappedPresenter,
DisplayArea.  It implements none of them, answered CLASS_E_CLASSNOTAVAILABLE,
the fallback was skipped, and the whole Microsoft.UI.Windowing class family was
permanently unavailable.  Measured with a probe that calls RoGetActivationFactory
and RoActivateInstance for each of them in this prefix:

    Microsoft.UI.Windowing.AppWindowTitleBar  RoGetActivationFactory = 80040111
    Microsoft.UI.Windowing.AppWindowPresenter RoGetActivationFactory = 80004002
    windows.ui.dll                            loaded as a consequence of the probe

The AppWindowTitleBar answer is windows.ui.dll's, the AppWindowPresenter answer
is the WinAppSDK module's own, and the difference is exactly the fall-through
that CLASS_E_CLASSNOTAVAILABLE suppresses.  After this change windows.ui.dll
returns E_NOINTERFACE, the Windows App SDK reaches CFlat::GetActivationFactory
for these classes, and the probe reports the fall-through happening.

The same chained-probe convention applies to every module that hands out a WinRT
factory, so the return value is corrected in all of them: twinapi.appcore,
windows.ui, windows.ui.xaml, windows.ui.core.textinput, windows.storage,
windows.storage.applicationdata, windows.applicationmodel,
windows.applicationmodel.calls, windows.web, windows.networking,
windows.networking.sockets, windows.networking.connectivity,
windows.networking.hostname, windows.media, windows.media.devices,
windows.media.mediacontrol, windows.media.speech, windows.media.playback.mediaplayer,
windows.media.playback.backgroundmediaplayer, windows.globalization,
windows.graphics, windows.devices.enumeration, windows.devices.bluetooth,
windows.devices.radios, windows.devices.usb, windows.gaming.input,
windows.gaming.ui.gamebar, windows.geolocation, windows.graphicscapture,
windows.hvsimanagementapi, windows.security.authentication.onlineid,
windows.security.credentials.ui.userconsentverifier, windows.system.profile.systemid,
windows.system.profile.systemmanufacturers, windows.perception.stub,
appxdeploymentclient, coremessaging, cryptowinrt, threadpoolwinrt and wintypes.
DllGetClassObject() keeps CLASS_E_CLASSNOTAVAILABLE, which is its documented
value for a CLSID.

The second half of the same apiset contract was missing entirely: the Windows
App SDK resolves Windowing_GetWindowFromWindowId, Windowing_GetWindowIdFromWindow,
Windowing_GetDisplayIdFromMonitor, Windowing_GetMonitorFromDisplayId,
Windowing_GetIconFromIconId and Windowing_GetIconIdFromIcon from it at run time
(Microsoft.Internal.FrameworkUdk.dll exports them from its windowing.cpp), and
windows.ui.dll exported none of them, so every resolution returned NULL.  They
are implemented in dlls/windows.ui/windowing.c: window and icon identifiers are
the handles themselves, which is well defined under Wine because both live in one
process-wide handle table, and display identifiers are the display monitor
enumeration index of the single display adapter a host presents.  All of them
validate the handle and fail on a window, icon or monitor that is not live.

A windows.ui:test_windowing suite covers the round trips (window, icon, display
monitor), the failure of an identifier taken from a destroyed window, the
argument checks, and the activation contract itself: DllGetActivationFactory()
for Windows.UI.ViewManagement.UISettings must return S_OK and
DllGetActivationFactory() for a class the module does not implement, such as
Microsoft.UI.Windowing.AppWindowTitleBar, must return E_NOINTERFACE with a NULL
factory.  It passes on x86_64 and i386.

Measured on WhatsApp Desktop 2.2620.102.0 in this prefix, 120 s runs, same tree
built both ways: the app reaches the same point in both, the log volume and the
combase error count are identical (734 lines, 240 combase errors, 7 CLASS_E
originations, 10 HybridDialog string lookups, WebView2Loader.dll never loaded,
no msedgewebview2.exe process).  The AppWindowTitleBar failure that remains in
the app does not come from this layer: the trace-pinned origin
Microsoft.UI.Windowing.dll!Api::IAppWindow::ExportAdapter::get_TitleBar+0x1ec45
calls the managed peer's interface dispatcher directly (System::Object::GetInterfaceDispatcher,
then dispatcher vtable slot 10) and never enters RoGetActivationFactory, so no
activation-factory change on the Wine side can reach it.  The remaining blocker
is the Windows App SDK windowing/title-bar implementation and the Windows.UI.
Composition layer behind it (Windows.UI.Composition.Compositor has no registry
entry and no implementation here, and Microsoft.UI.Windowing.Core.dll
dereferences the uninitialised member at +0x250 at Core.dll+0x38783 when the
title bar object is used).

